From: Luis Atala <luis.atala@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 20:55:05 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 0/4] HID: add driver for the Razer Barracuda X (2022) dongle

The Razer Barracuda X (2022) is a wireless headset with a 2.4 GHz USB
dongle (1532:0552). The dongle is a UAC1 audio device plus a HID
interface over which it tunnels vendor messages from the headset. Without
a driver, userspace cannot tell whether the headset is on, and does not
see its battery.

This series adds a driver for that HID interface. It registers a
device-scoped battery only while the headset is linked, sets the USB
wireless_status attribute, and reports the link as headset jack switches.

Patch 1 uses only what the dongle reports by itself, plus one link-state
query on probe and resume. Patch 2 asks the headset for its battery,
cable state and voltage. This requires routing the dongle's diagnostic
queries to the headset for the duration of the query, and restoring the
local route afterwards. It is a separate patch so that it can be reviewed,
or dropped, on its own.

Patch 3 adds a write-only headset_poweroff sysfs action. It sends a
hardware-validated MMI request only on an explicit write, serialized with
battery queries, and restores the local route. The protocol was tested
from userspace; the new driver entry point still needs hardware validation.

Patch 4 adds a serialized, allowlisted native-settings mailbox for explicitly
requested EQ, gaming, DND, standby and Quick Connect operations. It shares the
route mutex with battery queries and power-off. Default/Game EQ was physically
tested from userspace; the mailbox and other setters need hardware validation.

The driver is separate from hid-razer, which handles keyboard macro keys
and shares no protocol with this device.

Two userspace consumers use the link state:
- PipeWire hides a USB card while its wireless_status is "disconnected",
  from commit 03f894b ("alsa-udev: Add wireless device status
  monitoring"), which has not been released yet.
- The jack switches follow hid-playstation. A one-line snd-usb-audio
  patch reuses the DualSense jack quirk for this dongle, so that PipeWire
  falls back to another output while the headset is off. I will send it
  to alsa-devel once this driver is accepted.

Testing:
- KUnit: 13 tests after patch 1, 21 after patch 2, under QEMU x86_64
  with KASAN, UBSAN and lockdep. The complete series passes 26 tests,
  including startup serialization, failed-route recovery, stopping guards,
  power-off guards, native settings and read-length validation.
- W=1 and sparse (C=2) builds without warnings, built-in and as a module.
- Hardware: an out-of-tree version of this driver with the same protocol
  handling has been in use with a Razer Barracuda X (RZ04-04430-100) on
  v7.2.6. That covered headset power cycles, cable plug and unplug, the
  battery and cable GETs, and UPower and KDE listing the battery.
  Suspend and resume have not been tested on hardware. The latest startup
  serialization and route-recovery hardening also await hardware testing.

Razer does not document the protocol. It was derived from USB captures of
the vendor's Windows software and from the dongle firmware.

Tool use: most of the driver code and the commit messages were written
with an AI coding assistant (Claude Code, Anthropic) under my direction,
over several sessions. The sessions covered protocol analysis of my own
captures, the out-of-tree driver, and restructuring it for submission:
a single file, KUnit tests instead of a userspace test, a hid_is_usb()
check, and removal of the module parameters. checkpatch and sparse were
run on the result. OpenAI Codex assisted with the serialized power-off and
native-settings additions, startup serialization, route-recovery hardening,
their tests and protocol documentation.

